markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Why Apps Wait
  

Have you noticed a web app that feels like it “pauses” while it loads something—like a map, feed, or search results?

That’s your program waiting on work happening somewhere else (usually a network request).

<img src="https://images.code.org/1aca9c1c602c02f849d5182bc359154f-image-1770914096808.png" alt="icon of server, network, and clock symbolizing the time it takes for programs to run" width="300px" style="border-radius:10px; float:right; margin: 0 0 10px 10px;">

## What Is the Program Waiting For?

When your app loads data, it often has to ask something outside of itself for help:
- A server
- A database
- An API
- The network

Those requests take time.

The question is not **whether** there will be waiting.

**The real question is:**

> #### What should the program do *while* it waits?

<details style="background-color: #ececec; padding: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; border: 1px solid transparent; border-radius: 5px;">
    <summary style="font-size: 12pt; font-weight: bold;">🔎Check my thinking </summary>
<div style="margin-top: 20px; margin-left: 20px; margin-right: 20px; margin-bottom:75px;">
    <p>While the program waits for data, it should <b>show a loading message or animation</b> so users know the app is still working instead of frozen.</p>

</div>
</details>


---

## Synchronous vs. Asynchronous Programming

<div style="width:90%; margin:30px auto; text-align:center;">

<div style="display:inline-block; vertical-align:top;height:320px; width:300px; margin:20px 15px; background-color:#ffffff; color:#000000; padding:20px; border-radius:10px; box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); text-align:center;">
    <img src="https://images.code.org/d856a99241a595bfcac8d22abcfdbc4f-image-1770911888063.png" 
         alt="timeline showing async programming" style="width: 300px;height:150px;  margin-bottom: 10px;">
    <p style="font-size: 12pt; font-weight: bold; margin: 0;">Synchronous Programming</p>
    <p style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 10px;">Code that <strong>waits for one task to fully finish</strong> before moving on to the next.
      <br><br>
      Each step blocks the next step..</p>
</div>
 
 <!-- ASYNC CARD -->

  
<div style="display:inline-block; vertical-align:top; height:320px; width:300px; margin:20px 15px; background-color:#ffffff; color:#000000; padding:20px; border-radius:10px; box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); text-align:center;">
    <img src="https://images.code.org/55b6d1a0d9e431bce92e0f9eb621672b-image-1770912258147.png" 
         alt="timeline showing async programming" style="width: 300px; height:150px; margin-bottom: 10px;">
    <p style="font-size: 12pt; font-weight: bold; margin: 0;">Asynchronous Programming</p>
    <p style="font-size: 12pt; line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 10px;">  Code that <strong>continues doing other work</strong> while waiting for a task 
      (like loading data) to finish.
      <br><br>
      Waiting does not stop everything else.</p>
</div>

</div>



## Why This Matters
     
- **User experience:** Async helps apps stay responsive instead of freezing while they wait.
- **Correctness:** If you use data before it arrives, you get bugs (empty screens, undefined values, wrong results).
- **Performance:** Async lets you overlap “waiting time” with useful work.

 

<div style="background-color: #e4e2f8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 15px; margin: 20px auto; max-width: 85%; box-sizing: border-box; word-wrap: break-word; overflow-wrap: break-word;">
  <p style="font-size: 12pt; margin-top: 12px; line-height: 140%;">
    📖 <strong>Systems awareness</strong> is understanding how different parts of a system—like networks, servers, databases, and devices—work together, so you can design software that is reliable, responsive, and efficient.
  </p>
</div>

![]()


MARKDOWN
